<!-- ──────────────────────────────────────────── §01 ── -->
<section id="s-problem" data-code="problem">
<div class="section-no">01 — The Lock Problem</div>
<h2>Once Masked Diffusion Commits, It Can't Go Back</h2>
<p>Masked diffusion language models (MDLMs) like LLaDA work by starting with a sequence of <code>[MASK]</code> tokens and iteratively filling them in. The model fills a masked position, and that position is <em>locked</em> — it will never be updated again. This one-way commitment creates a rigid left-to-right-in-time structure, even if the model doesn't generate left-to-right in space.</p>
<p>The problem is easy to see: what if the model changes its mind? In natural language, later context often clarifies earlier words. If you lock "bank" before reading "fishing rod", you might never correct "financial institution" to "river bank". MDLMs have no mechanism for this.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">masked vs uniform diffusion — token commitment</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click to see how each paradigm fills tokens</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="compare-wrap" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr">
      <div class="compare-panel">
        <div class="compare-label label-red">⬛ Masked Diffusion — one-way locks</div>
        <div style="padding:.75rem">
          <div class="token-row" id="mdlm-row"></div>
          <div style="font-size:.68rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.5rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">🔒 committed tokens can never change</div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="compare-panel">
        <div class="compare-label label-green">✦ Uniform Diffusion (Sumi) — always revisable</div>
        <div style="padding:.75rem">
          <div class="token-row" id="udlm-row"></div>
          <div style="font-size:.68rem;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.5rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">↔ any token can update at any step</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="btn-row" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <button class="viz-btn" id="commit-step-btn">▶ Commit next token</button>
      <button class="viz-btn" id="commit-reset-btn">↺ Reset</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Uniform diffusion language models (UDLMs) relax this constraint entirely. Every token starts as random noise, and at every denoising step the model can revise <em>any</em> position — not just the ones that are still masked. In principle, this enables the model to self-correct, revisit commitments, and generate in a more global, holistic way.</p>
<p>The catch: no one had trained a UDLM from scratch at scale. The largest existing uniform diffusion models were either small (&lt;2B parameters) or adapted from pretrained AR models rather than trained natively on the diffusion objective. <strong>Sumi</strong> fills this gap: a 7B-parameter UDLM trained from scratch on 1.5T tokens, fully open.</p>
</section>

<!-- ──────────────────────────────────────────── §02 ── -->
<section id="s-uniform" data-code="uniform">
<div class="section-no">02 — Uniform Diffusion</div>
<h2>Any Token, Any Step: How GIDD Works</h2>
<p>Sumi is built on the <strong>GIDD</strong> (Generalized Interpolating Discrete Diffusion) framework. The core idea: at noise level <em>t</em>, each token position holds a mixture of its clean value and uniform random noise. As <em>t</em> decreases from 1→0, the signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) increases and tokens crystallize to their final values.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Forward process — adding noise</div>
  <span class="math-var">q</span>(z<sub>t</sub> | x) = Cat(z<sub>t</sub> ; <span class="math-var">α</span><sub>t</sub> · x + (1−<span class="math-var">α</span><sub>t</sub>) · <span class="math-var">u</span>)
  <div class="math-comment"># u = uniform noise; α_t decreases from 1 (clean) to 0 (pure noise) as t increases</div>
  <div class="math-comment"># log-SNR λ = log(α/(1-α)) restricted to [−9, 9]</div>
</div>

<p>The key difference from masked diffusion: there are no special <code>[MASK]</code> tokens. Every position holds an actual token at every step — it just might be a random, meaningless one. The model's job is to predict what the clean token should be, given all the noisy context around it.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">denoising trajectory — tokens crystallizing over time</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click Step to advance one denoising step</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="denoise-canvas" style="width:100%;height:200px;border-radius:8px;background:var(--card2)"></canvas>
    <div style="margin-top:.75rem;display:flex;gap:.5rem;align-items:center">
      <button class="viz-btn active" id="denoise-btn">▶ Step (t → 0)</button>
      <button class="viz-btn" id="denoise-reset-btn">↺ Reset (t = T)</button>
      <span id="denoise-label" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">step 0 / 8 — all noise</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>Crucially, Sumi uses a <strong>bidirectional</strong> Transformer — not a causal (left-to-right) one. At every denoising step, every position can attend to every other position. This means the model can see "fishing rod" when predicting "bank", even if "bank" appears earlier in the sequence. The architecture is a LLaMA-style block (36 layers, 4096 hidden, SwiGLU) with bidirectional attention and no causal mask.</p>
</section>

<!-- ──────────────────────────────────────────── §03 ── -->
<section id="s-canvas" data-code="canvas">
<div class="section-no">03 — The Canvas Model</div>
<h2>Fixed Canvas: 2048 Positions, Filled From Noise</h2>
<p>Sumi doesn't generate one token at a time, and it doesn't grow a sequence. Instead, it works on a fixed-size <strong>canvas</strong> — a buffer of 2048 token positions. At the start of generation, the entire canvas is filled with random noise tokens. The model then iteratively denoises this canvas until the answer region crystallizes.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">canvas layout — 2048-token buffer</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">hover regions to learn their role</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="canvas-layout" style="width:100%;height:120px;border-radius:8px"></canvas>
    <div id="canvas-tooltip" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted2);margin-top:.625rem;min-height:1.5rem"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The canvas has three regions. The <strong>prompt</strong> occupies the left positions — these are held fixed (the model doesn't update them). Then a boundary marker (<code>&lt;EOS&gt;&lt;BOS&gt;</code>) separates the prompt from the <strong>generation region</strong>, where token updates are permitted. The rest of the canvas is filled with random tokens that act as padding and are never scored.</p>
<p>Why 2048? The paper finds a "fluent band" — a range of canvas lengths where Sumi generates coherent text. Below ~1024 tokens, fluency degrades sharply on most tasks (GSM8K is especially sensitive). Above ~4096 tokens, generation also degrades. A canvas of 2048 sits comfortably in the fluent range for all evaluated tasks.</p>

<div class="canvas-note">📐 Canvas length vs generation length: the canvas is fixed at 2048 positions, but the generation region can be shorter — 512 for BBH, 64 for GSM8K, 256 for HumanEval/MBPP. The rest of the canvas is padding.</div>
</section>

<!-- ──────────────────────────────────────────── §04 ── -->
<section id="s-commit" data-code="commit">
<div class="section-no">04 — Confidence Sampling &amp; Commit Order</div>
<h2>The Model Self-Organizes Its Own Commitment Schedule</h2>
<p>UDLM generation is, by design, order-agnostic — the model can update any position at any step. So in what order does it actually commit tokens? The paper's most striking finding: the order depends entirely on <em>which sampler you use</em>.</p>
<p>Under <strong>ancestral sampling</strong> (standard diffusion sampling), tokens are committed essentially at random — no visible structure. Under <strong>confidence-based adaptive sampling</strong>, a self-organized order emerges: the model commits the positions it's most confident about first, and defers uncertain ones.</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">per-position commit order — color = which step that position crystallized</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">click to switch sampling mode</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <div class="btn-row">
      <button class="viz-btn" id="sampler-ancestral">Ancestral (random order)</button>
      <button class="viz-btn active" id="sampler-confidence">Adaptive / Confidence (structured)</button>
    </div>
    <div style="margin-bottom:.5rem;font-size:.68rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted)">
      Each cell = one token position. Color: <span style="color:#10b981">■</span> early commit → <span style="color:#8b5cf6">■</span> → <span style="color:#374151">■</span> late commit
    </div>
    <div id="commit-grid-wrap" style="overflow-x:auto"></div>
    <div id="commit-mode-label" style="font-size:.72rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono);color:var(--muted2);margin-top:.75rem"></div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>How does confidence sampling work? At each step, the sampler scores every noisy position by how much the model "wants" to change it: <em>score = max_token_probability − current_token_probability</em>. A high score means the model is confident that the current (noisy) token is wrong and should be replaced. The sampler commits the top-k highest-scoring positions each step.</p>

<div class="math-block">
  <div class="math-label">Confidence score per position</div>
  <span class="math-var">score</span>(i) = max<sub>z'</sub> p<sub>θ</sub>(x=z' | z<sub>t</sub>) − p<sub>θ</sub>(x=z<sub>t,i</sub> | z<sub>t</sub>)
  <div class="math-comment"># commit top-k positions where score is highest AND position is still noisy</div>
</div>

<p>This mechanism is order-agnostic by construction — it imposes no predetermined left-to-right or right-to-left order. Yet it produces <em>task-shaped structure</em>: for Python code, function signatures tend to commit early (the model is confident about <code>def</code> and <code>return</code>), while internal logic fills in later.</p>
</section>

<!-- ──────────────────────────────────────────── §05 ── -->
<section id="s-parallel" data-code="parallel">
<div class="section-no">05 — Parallel Decoding</div>
<h2>Commit k Tokens Per Step — Free Speedup on Coding Tasks</h2>
<p>Because the confidence sampler can commit any <em>k</em> tokens per step (not just one), there's a natural speedup knob: increase k. If accuracy holds up, you get a k× speedup in the number of denoising steps. The paper asks: how large can k be before accuracy degrades?</p>

<div class="vp">
  <div class="vp-head">
    <span class="vp-title">accuracy vs tokens committed per step</span>
    <span class="vp-hint">hover bars to see exact values</span>
  </div>
  <div class="vp-body">
    <canvas id="parallel-chart" style="width:100%;height:280px"></canvas>
    <div style="display:flex;gap:1.25rem;margin-top:.75rem;font-size:.7rem;font-family:var(--ff-mono)">
      <span style="color:#10b981">■ HumanEval (coding)</span>
      <span style="color:#f59e0b">■ GSM8K (math)</span>
      <span style="color:#8b5cf6">■ MBPP (coding)</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<p>The results are striking. For <strong>coding tasks</strong> (HumanEval, MBPP), accuracy stays within 1–2 percentage points of the k=1 baseline all the way to k=4. That's a 4× speedup with almost no quality loss. For <strong>math</strong> (GSM8K), the model is far more sensitive — accuracy already drops at k=2, and collapses quickly beyond that.</p>
<p>The interpretation: coding has more "obviously correct" local tokens (keywords, punctuation, common patterns), so the confidence sampler can safely batch-commit them. Multi-step arithmetic requires each intermediate result to influence all subsequent tokens — one wrong commit cascades.</p>

