    <tr><td>PIQA</td><td class="mn">66.4</td><td class="mn">78.7</td><td class="mn">79.8</td></tr>
    <tr><td>HellaSwag</td><td class="mn">60.0</td><td class="mn">76.2</td><td class="mn">75.6</td></tr>
    <tr><td>WinoGrande (5-shot)</td><td class="mn">60.0</td><td class="mn">74.7</td><td class="mn">71.3</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>The paper is candid: the commonsense gap is larger than data composition alone can explain, and attributing it fully to the education-heavy data mixture would require controlled experiments that weren't run. Understanding whether uniform diffusion <em>itself</em> has a structural disadvantage on commonsense tasks is left for future work.</p>
</section>

<!-- Listen-friendly content -->
<div class="listen-friendly-content" style="display: none;" aria-hidden="true">
  <div lang="en">
    <h2>Audio Summary: Sumi — First Open Uniform Diffusion Language Model at Scale</h2>
    <p>Sumi is a 7B-parameter uniform diffusion language model (UDLM) pretrained from scratch on 1.5T tokens — the first UDLM to reach both large parameter and token scale. Unlike masked diffusion models like LLaDA, which fill in blank tokens one by one and lock each commitment irreversibly, Sumi's uniform diffusion lets every token remain revisable at every denoising step. Sumi is built on the GIDD framework with a bidirectional transformer architecture: all positions attend to all others simultaneously, enabling the model to consider full context when updating any position.</p>
    <p>Sumi generates text on a fixed 2048-token canvas, starting from random noise and iteratively denoising. A key finding is that confidence-based adaptive sampling induces a self-organized commit order: the model naturally commits high-confidence positions first, producing task-shaped structure without any explicit ordering. This enables modest parallel decoding — committing up to 4 tokens per step on coding tasks with nearly no accuracy loss, a free 4x speedup. Benchmarks show Sumi leading comparable-budget AR models on knowledge, math, and coding, while showing a notable gap on commonsense reasoning tasks, likely due to its education-heavy training data.</p>
  </div>
  <div lang="zh">
    <h2>语音摘要：Sumi——首个大规模开放均匀扩散语言模型</h2>
    <p>Sumi是一个70亿参数的均匀扩散语言模型（UDLM），从头在1.5万亿token上预训练，是首个同时达到大参数规模和大token规模的UDLM。与LLaDA等掩码扩散模型不同——后者逐一填入空白token并永久锁定每次提交——Sumi的均匀扩散允许每个token在每个去噪步骤中保持可修改状态。Sumi基于GIDD框架，采用双向Transformer架构：所有位置相互注意，使模型在更新任意位置时都能考虑完整上下文。</p>
    <p>Sumi在固定的2048个token画布上生成文本，从随机噪声开始，迭代去噪。一个关键发现是：基于置信度的自适应采样能诱导出自组织的提交顺序——模型自然地先提交高置信度位置，无需任何显式排序便产生任务相关结构。这使得适度的并行解码成为可能——在代码任务上每步提交多达4个token几乎不损失准确率，实现免费的4倍加速。基准测试显示，Sumi在知识、数学和代码方面领先可比预算的AR模型，但在常识推理任务上有明显差距，可能源于其以教育内容为主的训练数据。</p>
